<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4024.16">
  <POU Name="FB_MasterAutoCycle" Id="{6b4ee376-0a0c-47a5-84cf-72c0a19ed5f4}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_MasterAutoCycle
(* Full-machine coordinated auto sequence spanning the robot and the 3
   pairs of SepSol / PushSol pistons (pair count fixed at 3 for this
   machine).

   Robot interface (TCP-only, 2026-07-26). The former discrete-I/O handshake
   is retired. All robot-side signals travel over FB_RobotTcpClient's
   STATE:n / CMD:n protocol:

     Robot -> PLC   nRobotCmd in {1,2}  (1=start, 2=reset error)
                    MAIN R_TRIGs on the edges; start pulse arrives here as
                    the VAR_INPUT bExtStartPulse.
     PLC -> Robot   nStateOut = TO_INT(eStep) or MANUAL(30)
                    Written by MAIN each scan, pushed by FB_RobotTcpClient
                    on change and as keep-alive.

   TWO KINDS OF START, WITH DIFFERENT OWNERS (2026-07-29):

     Operator START  = "home the pistons and arm the machine".
                       stHmi.bStart, from the HMI button or the green PB3 in
                       Automatic. Runs the INIT retract chain and stops in IDLE.
                       It does NOT run a bulb cycle.
     Robot CMD:1     = "run one bulb cycle". Arrives as bExtStartPulse. Valid
                       only from IDLE, i.e. only once a human has armed the
                       machine. bSimStartAssembly is its bench stand-in.

   So the machine cannot move after a power cycle, after Manual, or after a
   STOP until an operator presses START; and once armed, only the robot decides
   when a bulb runs. bContinuous is gone entirely (2026-08-06) -- it was written
   force-clears it every scan) -- it was the last path that could start motion
   with nothing external gating it.

   BENCH-BUILD EXCEPTION (2026-08-13, gated 2026-08-17): bTempTestContinuesAutoRun
   deliberately re-opens exactly that path, for log-file soak testing. It is kept
   for reuse on the next project rather than deleted, and it is therefore gated
   behind the VAR CONSTANT bBenchBuild, which is FALSE in any build that goes on
   a machine. Read bBenchBuild's comment before changing either.

   Sequence (all 3 Sep/Push pairs move in parallel; both grips move together):

     NOT_HOMED               In Automatic, NOT armed. Entered on power-up (the
                             declared default of stHmi.eStep), on any time in
                             Manual, and on operator STOP. Reports STATE:40,
                             which the robot reads as "wait". Exits only on
                             operator START -> INIT chain, bHomeThenIdle TRUE.
     IDLE                    Armed. Any of bExtStartPulse (robot CMD:1, and also
                             how the PB2+PB3 operator hold arrives), bStartCycle
                             (operator "run one bulb" from an HMI), bSimStartAssembly
                             (bench stand-in for the robot) or SimIdle -> INIT chain
                             with bHomeThenIdle FALSE (a bulb cycle). Four ways in,
                             logged distinctly so the ring says which.
                             Operator START here is IGNORED (2026-08-05).
                             It used to re-home and come back, which was a no-op
                             in outcome and let a green-before-orange press of
                             the PB2+PB3 combo lose its bulb request: the combo's
                             one-scan pulse landed mid INIT_* chain, where
                             nothing consumes it. Section 0b logs the refusal.
     INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING    drive PushCmdRetract[1..3]; wait AND of all
                             PushIsRetracted. Guarantees pistons are home before
                             a cycle starts, even if a prior manual move left
                             them extended.
     INIT_SEP_RETRACTING     drive SepCmdRetract[1..3]; wait AND of all
                             SepIsRetracted. Push must retract first (see INIT
                             above) because sep retract with push extended is
                             the same collision case avoided end-of-cycle.
     INIT_GRIP_RETRACTING    drive GripCmdRetract[1..2]; wait bGripIsRetracted[1]
                             OR [2] -- the same OR deviation as the two GRIP
                             states below, so error 8 shares their blind spot:
                             it fires only if BOTH grippers fail to come home.
                             Defensive symmetry with the INIT
                             Sep/Push states, so a manual jog left extended
                             does not collide with the arriving plate.
                             THE ONE PLACE THE TWO CALLERS DIVERGE: exits to
                             IDLE when bHomeThenIdle, else to CHECK_PLATE.
     CHECK_PLATE              wait bPlateOk = bPlateSenL OR bPlateSenR (robot
                             has placed the plate on the fixture). OR, not AND:
                             only one plate sensor is confirmed wired, so an AND
                             would fault every cycle. Deliberate field deviation
                             -- see the plate/grip note in CLAUDE.md before
                             "fixing" it. Latched at start only;
                             sensors are not monitored past this step.
                             stCfg.bBypassPlateSensors removes the timeout
                             ERROR only -- the sensor fast-path still applies,
                             and on expiry the cycle continues instead of
                             raising error 9. bNoSensors does NOT cover this
                             state.
     GRIP_EXTENDING          drive GripCmdExtend[1..2]; wait bGripIsExtended[1]
                             OR [2] -- again OR, not AND, because GripSolR has
                             no air on the machine. COST: with either sensor
                             satisfying it, a genuine single-gripper failure is
                             invisible and the cycle can run on a plate clamped
                             from one side; error 10 fires only if BOTH fail.
     SEP_EXTENDING           drive SepCmdExtend[1..3]; wait AND of all SepIsExtended.
                             Grip stays extended for every state below.
     PUSH_EXTENDING          drive PushCmdExtend[1..3]; wait AND of all PushIsExtended
     DWELL_PUSH              hold both extended; wait tDwellPushMs
     PUSH_RETRACTING         drive PushCmdRetract[1..3]; wait AND of all PushIsRetracted
     PUSH_RETRACTED_DWELL    wait tPushRetractedDwellMs
     SEP_RETRACTING          drive SepCmdRetract[1..3]; wait AND of all SepIsRetracted
     SEP_RETRACTED_DWELL     wait tSepRetractedDwellMs
     GRIP_RETRACTING         drive GripCmdRetract[1..2]; wait bGripIsRetracted[1]
                             OR [2] (same OR deviation as GRIP_EXTENDING, and
                             error 11 has the same blind spot). Releases the
                             plate; increment nCyclesCompleted; back to IDLE --
                             still armed, so the robot's next CMD:1 runs another.
     ERR                     latched; iErrorCode set to step code. Reports
                             STATE:99, on which the robot answers CMD:2. RESET
                             (that CMD:2, the HMI button, or the orange PB2)
                             clears the error and enters the RECOVER_* chain
                             below -- NOT the INIT chain, and NOT straight to
                             IDLE. A fault leaves the pistons anywhere, so
                             advertising IDLE(0) would claim ready while it is
                             not, and RESET landing in IDLE with the robot still
                             asking made RESET itself a start button.
     RECOVER_PUSH_RETR       Fault-recovery retract chain (50/51/52), added
     RECOVER_SEP_RETR        2026-08-05. Same motion and the same collision
     RECOVER_GRIP_RETR       ordering as the INIT chain, but its own identity so
                             a failed RECOVERY is distinguishable from a failed
                             ARMING: error codes 12/13/14 instead of 6/7/8, and
                             its own sStepText. Entered ONLY from ERR on RESET.
                             RECOVER_GRIP_RETR always exits to IDLE -- by then
                             the machine really is homed, so reporting STATE:0 is
                             honest. The robot needed no new branch: it acts on 0
                             and 99 and treats everything else as "wait".

   Error codes:
     0  = OK
     1  = SEP_EXTENDING timed out
     3  = PUSH_EXTENDING timed out
     4  = PUSH_RETRACTING timed out
     5  = SEP_RETRACTING timed out
     6  = INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING timed out
     7  = INIT_SEP_RETRACTING timed out
     8  = INIT_GRIP_RETRACTING timed out
     9  = CHECK_PLATE timed out (plate never arrived / one sensor never TRUE)
          -- suppressed while stCfg.bBypassPlateSensors is set
    10  = GRIP_EXTENDING timed out
    11  = GRIP_RETRACTING timed out
    12  = RECOVER_PUSH_RETR timed out  -- recovery, deliberately distinct from 6
    13  = RECOVER_SEP_RETR timed out   -- distinct from 7
    14  = RECOVER_GRIP_RETR timed out  -- distinct from 8
    99  = RETIRED (was 'Operator STOP while running'). STOP now disarms to
          NOT_HOMED instead of faulting -- see Section 2. Do not reuse the
          number: old log entries and CSV exports still carry it.

   The former WAIT_POS2 state (error code 2) is retired: the robot code
   (src0.lua PerformStandSequence) already moves the arm clear of the
   stand BEFORE raising STAND_SEQ_DO, so the PLC does not need a separate
   "robot moved away" wait between SEP_EXTENDING and PUSH_EXTENDING. The
   E_MasterAutoStep.WAIT_POS2 enum value and stHmi.bSimStep_WaitPos2 flag
   remain declared but are unused; safe to remove once the HMI is cleaned.

   Per-step Sim flags on stHmi (bSimStep_Idle, bSimStep_SepExt, and so on)
   let a bench operator advance any waiting step without physical sensors.
   Rising-edge triggered; the FB auto-clears the flag after consumption.

   Sensorless mode (stCfg.bNoSensors): the TWELVE movement states ignore
   sensor feedback and advance when tStepTimeoutMs expires — the timeout
   doubles as the fixed step duration. Those twelve are both INIT_* sets
   (3), the three RECOVER_* states, both GRIP states and the four Sep/Push
   states. So error codes 1/3/4/5/6/7/8/10/11/12/13/14 are ALL unreachable
   while sensorless — this said "the 4 movement states" and "1/3/4/5",
   which was wrong before the RECOVER chain existed and is wronger now.
   CHECK_PLATE is deliberately NOT covered: it keeps its own
   tPlateWaitTimeoutMs and still needs a plate sensor, so error 9 stays
   reachable. Every bench run so far has had this flag set, which is why no
   movement-timeout path has ever been exercised on a panel. *)
VAR_INPUT
    (* External start pulse (rising-edge; one scan wide). MAIN fires this
       when the robot's CMD:1 arrives via TCP, or a physical PB1 rising
       edge in Auto mode. HMI START button and SimStartAssembly are
       consumed separately below via their own R_TRIGs. *)
    bExtStartPulse        : BOOL;

    (* Global machine-mode gate (GVL_HMI.bAutoMode). While FALSE the cycle is
       held in IDLE: every start trigger is ignored and a running cycle is
       aborted back to IDLE. This is the hard interlock that stops the
       operator or a stray robot edge starting motion while the machine is
       in Manual. *)
    bMachineAuto          : BOOL;

    (* BENCH SOAK FLAG -- log-file testing only. Wired by MAIN from
       GVL_HMI.bTempTestContinuesAutoRun, ticked online from the IDE. No HMI
       control by decision: an operator must not be able to reach it.

       While TRUE -- AND the build is a bench build, see bBenchBuild -- the
       machine self-arms out of NOT_HOMED and starts another bulb every time it
       reaches IDLE, with no operator START, no robot CMD:1 and no sim flag. So
       the log ring and the CSV fill continuously and rotation / retention can
       actually be exercised on a panel.

       LEVEL, not an edge, unlike every other term it is OR-ed into. That is the
       point: it has to re-fire on each arrival in IDLE.

       Three things it deliberately does NOT do, even on a bench build:
         - it does not bypass bMachineAuto, so Manual is still the hard stop;
         - it does not touch ERR, so a fault still ends the run (with bNoSensors
           AND bBypassPlateSensors set, no error code is reachable, so a soak
           should not fault at all -- if it does, that is a real finding);
         - it does not persist. GVL_HMI is plain VAR_GLOBAL, so the FALSE
           initialiser is re-applied on every cold boot and every download.

       NOTHING downstream reads this input directly. It is gated into
       bTempTestRun in Section 0a and every use site reads that. *)
    bTempTestContinuesAutoRun : BOOL;

    (* Per-pair sensor state. MAIN wires each element from stSepSolK/stPushSolK.bIs*. *)
    bSepIsExtended        : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;
    bSepIsRetracted       : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;
    bPushIsExtended       : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;
    bPushIsRetracted      : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;

    (* Grip pistons — L=[1], R=[2]. MAIN wires from stGripSolL/R.bIs*. *)
    bGripIsExtended       : ARRAY[1..2] OF BOOL;
    bGripIsRetracted      : ARRAY[1..2] OF BOOL;

    (* Plate-presence sensors gating CHECK_PLATE. Both must be TRUE before the
       cycle can advance out of CHECK_PLATE into GRIP_EXTENDING. Latched at
       cycle start only — not monitored past that step. *)
    bPlateSenL            : BOOL;
    bPlateSenR            : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR_IN_OUT
    stHmi     : ST_HmiMasterAuto;
    stCfg     : ST_HmiMasterAutoCfg;
    stRobot   : ST_HmiRobot;
END_VAR

VAR_OUTPUT
    (* MAIN OR-merges these with the per-piston auto FBs. *)
    bSepCmdExtend         : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;
    bSepCmdRetract        : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;
    bPushCmdExtend        : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;
    bPushCmdRetract       : ARRAY[1..3] OF BOOL;

    (* Grip piston commands. Extend held TRUE from GRIP_EXTENDING through
       SEP_RETRACTED_DWELL so the plate stays clamped for the entire cycle. *)
    bGripCmdExtend        : ARRAY[1..2] OF BOOL;
    bGripCmdRetract       : ARRAY[1..2] OF BOOL;

    (* TRUE while the master cycle is in any state other than IDLE or ERR.
       MAIN reads this to force enabled pistons' stHmi.eMode := Automatic
       while running, so bAutoCmd* actually reaches the piston coil (in
       Manual mode the piston FB ignores bAutoCmd*)
    bAnyRunning           : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR
    (* Rising-edge detectors for momentary HMI buttons + robot start signal. *)
    fbTrigStart           : R_TRIG;
    fbTrigStop            : R_TRIG;
    fbTrigReset           : R_TRIG;
    fbTrigStartAssembly   : R_TRIG;
    fbTrigStartCycle      : R_TRIG;

    (* TEMPORARY -- mode-change log for the CSV soak-test flag. Delete with it. *)
    fbTrigTempTestOn      : R_TRIG;
    fbTrigTempTestOff     : F_TRIG;

    (* Rising-edge detectors for per-step Sim flags. *)
    fbSim_Idle            : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_InitPushRet     : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_InitSepRet      : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_InitGripRet     : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_WaitPlate       : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_GripExt         : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_SepExt          : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_PushExt         : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_DwellPush       : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_PushRet         : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_PushRetDwell    : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_SepRet          : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_SepRetDwell     : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_GripRet         : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_RecoverPushRet  : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_RecoverSepRet   : R_TRIG;
    fbSim_RecoverGripRet  : R_TRIG;

    (* Which exit the INIT retract chain takes when it completes:
         TRUE  -> IDLE         the chain was run to HOME the machine
                               (operator START, or recovery from ERR)
         FALSE -> CHECK_PLATE   the chain is the front of a bulb cycle
                               (robot CMD:1)
       Set by whoever ENTERS the chain, never from inside it -- the three INIT
       states are shared by both callers and must not care which one they serve. *)
    bHomeThenIdle         : BOOL;

    (* Per-step timer. Same TON, different PT depending on state. On DWELL
       states PT is the dwell; on wait states PT is the timeout. *)
    tonStep               : TON;

    (* Transition detect for the timer reset and the transition log. Seeded to
       NOT_HOMED to match stHmi.eStep's own default, so power-up does not log a
       phantom 'IDLE -> NOT_HOMED' for a state the machine was never in. *)
    ePrevStep             : E_MasterAutoStep := E_MasterAutoStep.NOT_HOMED;

    (* Loop index *)
    i                     : USINT;

    (* Aggregate sensor flags, computed each cycle in Section 1. *)
    bAllSepExtended       : BOOL;
    bAllSepRetracted      : BOOL;
    bAllPushExtended      : BOOL;
    bAllPushRetracted     : BOOL;
    bAllGripExtended      : BOOL;
    bAllGripRetracted     : BOOL;
    bPlateOk              : BOOL;

    (* Gated form of bTempTestContinuesAutoRun: the raw flag AND bBenchBuild.
       Every use site reads THIS and never the raw input, so the gate has exactly
       one application point and a future use site cannot forget it. *)
    bTempTestRun          : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR CONSTANT
    (* BENCH-BUILD GATE for bTempTestContinuesAutoRun. FALSE in every build that
       goes on a production machine. Set TRUE only in an IDE session driving a
       bench panel, and set it back before the build that ships.

       Why a CONSTANT rather than another runtime flag: GVL_HMI carries
       TcHmiSymbol.AddSymbol, so everything in it is writable over ADS by
       FlowerPyHmi, by pb_test, or by any laptop with a route. "It is only ever
       set from the IDE" is a habit, not an interlock -- the same reasoning the
       CASE ELSE below applies to eStep. A VAR CONSTANT cannot be written at
       runtime by anything, so while this is FALSE the self-start path is
       provably unreachable no matter what lands in the GVL field.

       WHY THE PATH MUST NOT SHIP ENABLED. It starts a bulb without the robot's
       CMD:1, and CMD:1 is the ONLY guarantee the PLC has that the arm is clear
       of the stand: WAIT_POS2 was retired on exactly that basis (see the header
       note), so there is no independent check left. CHECK_PLATE is not a
       substitute -- plate-present says the plate was placed, not that the arm
       withdrew, and it is an OR on the one confirmed sensor, latched at entry.
       Second, it self-arms out of NOT_HOMED, which re-opens the Manual->Auto
       hazard the 2026-07-29 arming model closed: the START press after Manual is
       the operator confirming they are clear of the machine, and this skips it.
       Neither of those is fixed by the flag being volatile -- both are reachable
       inside a single power cycle. *)
    bBenchBuild : BOOL := FALSE;
END_VAR]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[(* ==========================================
   0a. RISING-EDGE DETECTORS
   ========================================== *)
fbTrigStart        (CLK := stHmi.bStart);
fbTrigStop         (CLK := stHmi.bStop);
fbTrigReset        (CLK := stHmi.bReset);
fbTrigStartAssembly(CLK := stHmi.bSimStartAssembly);
fbTrigStartCycle   (CLK := stHmi.bStartCycle);

(* Bench soak flag. THE GATE IS APPLIED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE -- everything
   downstream reads bTempTestRun, so on a production build (bBenchBuild FALSE)
   it is FALSE whatever an ADS client writes to the GVL field.

   The two edge detectors deliberately watch the RAW flag, not the gated one, so
   that an attempt to enable it on a production build is still visible in the log
   instead of vanishing silently. *)
bTempTestRun := bBenchBuild AND bTempTestContinuesAutoRun;

fbTrigTempTestOn   (CLK := bTempTestContinuesAutoRun);
fbTrigTempTestOff  (CLK := bTempTestContinuesAutoRun);

fbSim_Idle        (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_Idle);
fbSim_InitPushRet (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_InitPushRet);
fbSim_InitSepRet  (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_InitSepRet);
fbSim_InitGripRet (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_InitGripRet);
fbSim_WaitPlate   (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_WaitPlate);
fbSim_GripExt     (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_GripExt);
fbSim_SepExt      (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_SepExt);
fbSim_PushExt     (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_PushExt);
fbSim_DwellPush   (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_DwellPush);
fbSim_PushRet     (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_PushRet);
fbSim_PushRetDwell(CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_PushRetDwell);
fbSim_SepRet      (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_SepRet);
fbSim_SepRetDwell (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_SepRetDwell);
fbSim_GripRet     (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_GripRet);
fbSim_RecoverPushRet(CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_RecoverPushRet);
fbSim_RecoverSepRet (CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_RecoverSepRet);
fbSim_RecoverGripRet(CLK := stHmi.bSimStep_RecoverGripRet);

(* ==========================================
   0b. AUTO-CLEAR ONE-SHOT FLAGS ON RISING EDGE
   HMI button flags and Sim flags are momentary; after we detect the
   rising edge we clear the raw flag so the operator's next click can
   fire again. bExtStartPulse is already a one-scan pulse produced by
   MAIN's R_TRIG on the robot's TCP command channel -- nothing to clear.
   ========================================== *)
IF fbTrigStart.Q  THEN
    stHmi.bStart := FALSE;
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name, sMsg := 'START pressed');
END_IF;
IF fbTrigStop.Q   THEN
    stHmi.bStop  := FALSE;
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name, sMsg := 'STOP pressed');
END_IF;
IF fbTrigReset.Q  THEN
    stHmi.bReset := FALSE;
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name, sMsg := 'RESET pressed');
END_IF;

(* Bench sim of the robot start: fires on the HMI checkbox rising edge and
   auto-clears. Log so bench-driven cycles show up in the event log. The
   robot-driven start comes in as bExtStartPulse (already an edge, no R_TRIG
   needed here) and is logged from MAIN when nRobotCmd goes 0->1. *)
IF fbTrigStartAssembly.Q THEN
    stHmi.bSimStartAssembly := FALSE;
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name, sMsg := 'Sim start pressed');
END_IF;
(* Operator cycle start. Logged with its own message so the ring tells you
   WHO asked for the bulb: this, 'Sim start pressed' (bench stand-in for the
   robot), 'External start pulse' (the real robot, or the PB2+PB3 hold, both
   arriving as bExtStartPulse) and 'START pressed' (arming) are four
   different events and a fault report needs to tell them apart. *)
IF fbTrigStartCycle.Q THEN
    stHmi.bStartCycle := FALSE;
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name,
               sMsg := 'Operator cycle start');
END_IF;
IF bExtStartPulse THEN
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name, sMsg := 'External start pulse');
END_IF;

(* Bench soak flag, mode change. WARN on enable rather than INFO because a CSV
   read back weeks later has to say plainly that from this line onward the
   machine was self-running and every bulb below it is a test artefact, not
   production output.

   The refusal is logged too, and at WARN. On a production build the flag does
   nothing, and somebody who has just set it and is watching an idle machine
   needs to be told WHY rather than left to conclude the panel is broken -- this
   is the one line that says "rebuild with bBenchBuild TRUE". *)
IF fbTrigTempTestOn.Q THEN
    IF bBenchBuild THEN
        F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.WARN, sSource := stHmi.Name,
                   sMsg := 'BENCH SOAK continuous auto-run ENABLED');
    ELSE
        F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.WARN, sSource := stHmi.Name,
                   sMsg := 'Bench soak flag IGNORED - not a bench build');
    END_IF;
END_IF;
IF fbTrigTempTestOff.Q AND bBenchBuild THEN
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name,
               sMsg := 'BENCH SOAK continuous auto-run disabled');
END_IF;

IF fbSim_Idle.Q         THEN stHmi.bSimStep_Idle         := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_InitPushRet.Q  THEN stHmi.bSimStep_InitPushRet  := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_InitSepRet.Q   THEN stHmi.bSimStep_InitSepRet   := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_InitGripRet.Q  THEN stHmi.bSimStep_InitGripRet  := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_WaitPlate.Q    THEN stHmi.bSimStep_WaitPlate    := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_GripExt.Q      THEN stHmi.bSimStep_GripExt      := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_SepExt.Q       THEN stHmi.bSimStep_SepExt       := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_PushExt.Q      THEN stHmi.bSimStep_PushExt      := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_DwellPush.Q    THEN stHmi.bSimStep_DwellPush    := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_PushRet.Q      THEN stHmi.bSimStep_PushRet      := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_PushRetDwell.Q THEN stHmi.bSimStep_PushRetDwell := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_SepRet.Q       THEN stHmi.bSimStep_SepRet       := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_SepRetDwell.Q  THEN stHmi.bSimStep_SepRetDwell  := FALSE; END_IF;
IF fbSim_GripRet.Q      THEN stHmi.bSimStep_GripRet      := FALSE; END_IF;

(* ==========================================
   1. AGGREGATE SENSOR STATE
   ========================================== *)
bAllSepExtended   := bSepIsExtended[1]   AND bSepIsExtended[2]   AND bSepIsExtended[3];
bAllSepRetracted  := bSepIsRetracted[1]  AND bSepIsRetracted[2]  AND bSepIsRetracted[3];
bAllPushExtended  := bPushIsExtended[1]  AND bPushIsExtended[2]  AND bPushIsExtended[3];
bAllPushRetracted := bPushIsRetracted[1] AND bPushIsRetracted[2] AND bPushIsRetracted[3];
bAllGripExtended  := bGripIsExtended[1]  OR bGripIsExtended[2];
bAllGripRetracted := bGripIsRetracted[1] OR bGripIsRetracted[2];
bPlateOk          := bPlateSenL OR bPlateSenR;

(* ==========================================
   2. GLOBAL OVERRIDES — STOP and RESET
   Both were rewritten 2026-07-29; this header used to describe the old
   behaviour ("STOP latches error 99 and drops to ERR, RESET returns to
   IDLE", plus a discrete-IO RESET_ALARM on robot DI 10 that no longer
   exists). What the three branches below actually do now:

     STOP   -> NOT_HOMED, error cleared. DISARMS, does not fault. Fires from
               IDLE too, giving the operator an explicit disarm.
     RESET  -> clears the error and enters the INIT homing chain with
               bHomeThenIdle TRUE. NOT straight to IDLE.
     Manual -> parks in NOT_HOMED (ERR stays latched), which is the whole
               implementation of "returning from Manual needs a START".

   RESET arrives either as the HMI button or as the robot's CMD:2, which MAIN
   fans into the same stHmi.bReset field — the robot resets the fault over
   TCP now, so there is nothing for the operator to reset on the robot side.
   ========================================== *)
IF fbTrigStop.Q AND stHmi.eStep <> E_MasterAutoStep.ERR THEN
    (* STOP DISARMS, it does not fault. Error code 99 ('Operator STOP while
       running') is retired.

       It used to latch ERR, which was harmless only while the CMD:1 latch bug
       masked it. Now that a re-sent command is honoured, ERR means the robot
       sees STATE:99, answers CMD:2, the PLC self-homes and reports IDLE, and
       the robot immediately starts another bulb -- i.e. a deliberate operator
       STOP would restart the machine within a couple of seconds.

       Dropping to NOT_HOMED instead reports 'wait' rather than 'fault', so the
       robot never tries to reset it, and the machine cannot move again until a
       human presses START. It also restores the distinction that ERR means
       something went WRONG, not that someone intervened.

       Fires from IDLE too, which gives the operator an explicit disarm: STOP in
       IDLE takes the machine out of the robot's reach. *)
    stHmi.iErrorCode := 0;
    stHmi.sErrorText := '';
    stHmi.eStep      := E_MasterAutoStep.NOT_HOMED;
END_IF;

IF fbTrigReset.Q AND stHmi.eStep = E_MasterAutoStep.ERR THEN
    (* RESET (operator button, or the robot's CMD:2 fanned in by MAIN) clears
       the fault and HOMES -- it does not drop straight to IDLE any more.

       Two reasons. A fault leaves the pistons in unknown positions, so going
       to IDLE would advertise STATE:0 ('ready') while the machine is not; and
       RESET landing in IDLE with the robot still asking for a bulb made RESET
       itself a start button, ~1 s after the operator cleared the fault. Now
       RESET only ever produces motion towards home, and the cycle starts on the
       robot's own CMD:1 once IDLE is genuinely reached. *)
    stHmi.iErrorCode := 0;
    stHmi.sErrorText := '';
    (* Recovery runs its OWN chain now, not the shared RETRACT/INIT one,
       so a failed recovery reports codes 12/13/14 and a distinct
       sStepText instead of looking exactly like a failed arming.
       bHomeThenIdle is irrelevant to that chain -- RECOVER_GRIP_RETR
       always exits to IDLE. *)
    stHmi.eStep      := E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_PUSH_RETR;
END_IF;

(* Global Auto/Manual hard interlock. Being in Manual forces the cycle out of
   any running state and drops Continuous, so a stray robot start pulse cannot
   restart motion. Not treated as a fault (no ERR/error code) — Manual is a
   normal operator choice, not an alarm. ERR stays latched until RESET.

   Parks in NOT_HOMED rather than IDLE, and THAT is the whole implementation of
   "returning from Manual needs an operator START": manual jogging can leave the
   pistons anywhere, so the machine must be un-armed when Auto comes back. No
   Manual->Auto edge detection is needed — the state is simply already
   NOT_HOMED, and the operator's START homes it. *)
IF NOT bMachineAuto AND stHmi.eStep <> E_MasterAutoStep.NOT_HOMED AND stHmi.eStep <> E_MasterAutoStep.ERR THEN
    stHmi.eStep       := E_MasterAutoStep.NOT_HOMED;
END_IF;

(* ==========================================
   3. STEP TIMER + TRANSITION LOG
   Reset tonStep on any state change. In DWELL states PT is the dwell
   duration and Q rising = "dwell complete, advance". In wait states PT
   is the timeout and Q rising = "timed out, error". Non-waiting states
   (IDLE, ERR) do not consult Q.

   Also emit one log entry per transition — INFO with "PREV -> NEW" for
   normal advances, ERR with sErrorText when the new state is ERR. The
   check runs one scan AFTER Section 4 set eStep, so sErrorText and
   iErrorCode are already populated. Order matters: log BEFORE updating
   ePrevStep so both endpoints are still available.
   ========================================== *)
IF stHmi.eStep <> ePrevStep THEN
    IF stHmi.eStep = E_MasterAutoStep.ERR THEN
        F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.ERR, sSource := stHmi.Name, sMsg := stHmi.sErrorText);
    ELSE
        F_LogEvent(
            eSev    := E_LogSev.INFO,
            sSource := stHmi.Name,
            sMsg    := CONCAT(CONCAT(TO_STRING(ePrevStep), ' -> '), TO_STRING(stHmi.eStep))
        );
    END_IF

    tonStep(IN := FALSE, PT := T#0MS);   // pulse-off resets ET to 0
    ePrevStep := stHmi.eStep;
END_IF;

(* PT depends on state. Wait states use tStepTimeoutMs; dwell states use their own.
   CHECK_PLATE uses tPlateWaitTimeoutMs (bounded by robot placement time). *)
CASE stHmi.eStep OF
    E_MasterAutoStep.DWELL_PUSH:
        tonStep(IN := TRUE, PT := UDINT_TO_TIME(stCfg.tDwellPushMs));
    E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_RETRACTED_DWELL:
        tonStep(IN := TRUE, PT := UDINT_TO_TIME(stCfg.tPushRetractedDwellMs));
    E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_RETRACTED_DWELL:
        tonStep(IN := TRUE, PT := UDINT_TO_TIME(stCfg.tSepRetractedDwellMs));
    E_MasterAutoStep.CHECK_PLATE:
        tonStep(IN := TRUE, PT := UDINT_TO_TIME(stCfg.tPlateWaitTimeoutMs));
    E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_EXTENDING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_EXTENDING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_RETRACTING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_RETRACTING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_SEP_RETRACTING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_GRIP_RETRACTING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.GRIP_EXTENDING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.GRIP_RETRACTING,
    E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_PUSH_RETR,
    E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_SEP_RETR,
    E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_GRIP_RETR:
        tonStep(IN := TRUE, PT := UDINT_TO_TIME(stCfg.tStepTimeoutMs));
ELSE
    tonStep(IN := FALSE, PT := T#0MS);
END_CASE;

stHmi.tElapsedS := DINT_TO_LREAL(TIME_TO_DINT(tonStep.ET)) / 1000.0;

(* ==========================================
   4. STATE MACHINE
   Default: clear all commands each cycle in the ELSE-ish paths so a
   dropped state doesn't leave stale coils driven. Each state re-asserts
   the commands it owns. Grip commands are held TRUE from GRIP_EXTENDING
   through SEP_RETRACTED_DWELL so the plate stays clamped for the entire
   Sep/Push cycle.
   ========================================== *)
CASE stHmi.eStep OF

    E_MasterAutoStep.IDLE:
        ResetAllCommands();
        (* No active error while in IDLE (unless just about to enter ERR
           via a global override, handled above). *)
        stHmi.iErrorCode := 0;
        stHmi.sErrorText := '';

        (* ONLY THE ROBOT STARTS A BULB CYCLE.

           fbTrigStart.Q (operator START) and the retired stCfg.bContinuous were
           removed from this condition. That is the point of the whole arming
           model: START means "home and arm", and Continuous would re-arm with
           nothing external gating it at all. What is left is the robot's CMD:1
           (arriving as bExtStartPulse -- which is also how the PB2+PB3 hold
           arrives), the operator's own bStartCycle, its bench stand-in
           bSimStartAssembly, and the per-step sim flag.

           bStartCycle is a SEPARATE field from bStart on purpose, and the
           distinction is the whole reason START was removed from here: bStart
           means "home and arm" in NOT_HOMED, so overloading it to also mean
           "run a bulb" in IDLE would restore the two-meanings-one-symbol
           problem that let a green-before-orange combo press lose its
           request. A separate command cannot be misread by state.

           Still gated by bMachineAuto: in Manual nothing starts, even on a
           robot pulse (no double-control).

           Enters the INIT chain rather than CHECK_PLATE directly so pistons left
           somewhere unexpected are driven home first; with everything already
           retracted the three INIT states self-satisfy on the next scan, so it
           costs nothing per bulb. bHomeThenIdle FALSE routes the chain's exit
           on to CHECK_PLATE and the rest of the cycle. *)
        (* bTempTestRun is OR-ed in as a LEVEL, so the moment the machine lands
           back in IDLE it starts the next bulb -- that is what makes a soak
           self-sustaining. Compile-time FALSE unless bBenchBuild is set, so this
           term does not exist on a production build. *)
        IF bMachineAuto AND (bExtStartPulse OR fbTrigStartCycle.Q
                             OR fbTrigStartAssembly.Q OR fbSim_Idle.Q
                             OR bTempTestRun) THEN
            (* Logged per cycle, not just once when the flag goes TRUE, because
               aRecent is only 20 slots deep -- the enable WARN scrolls out of
               the panel ring within two bulbs, and without this line the Logs
               page would show bulb after bulb with nothing saying what started
               them. In the CSV it is redundant with the WARN; on the panel it is
               the only answer.

               Gated, not raw: on a production build a genuine robot-started bulb
               must not be labelled a soak cycle just because someone set the GVL
               field over ADS. *)
            IF bTempTestRun THEN
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := stHmi.Name,
                           sMsg := 'BENCH SOAK auto-run cycle start');
            END_IF;
            bHomeThenIdle := FALSE;
            stHmi.eStep   := E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING;
        END_IF;

        (* OPERATOR START IS IGNORED HERE (2026-08-05). It used to re-home and
           come back, justified as "harmless, and it keeps START's meaning
           identical in every state". Removed for two reasons.

           It was a no-op in outcome. Nothing can disturb a piston while we sit
           in IDLE: each valve output has exactly one writer -- its piston FB's
           bOutSolenoid -- and in Automatic the auto commands are all FALSE here,
           the HMI Extend/Retract selections are force-cleared every scan, and
           the PB jogs are not available in any Auto state. IDLE is only reached
           from INIT_GRIP_RETRACTING, GRIP_RETRACTING or RECOVER_GRIP_RETR, all
           of which end with everything retracted. So the pistons were already
           home and the INIT chain self-satisfied.

           And it cost a real defect. Because the re-home left IDLE, an operator
           pressing GREEN before ORANGE for the PB2+PB3 bulb-start combo could
           have the combo's one-scan bExtStartPulse land while eStep was still in
           the INIT chain -- where nothing consumes it -- so the request was
           silently dropped and no bulb ran. Measured on the machine. With START
           ignored here the machine never leaves IDLE, so that pulse always lands
           somewhere that consumes it. This removes the cause rather than
           tolerating it.

           The position-proof the re-home offered is still reachable, just
           deliberately: STOP (red PB held) drops to NOT_HOMED, and START from
           there homes and re-arms.

           fbTrigStart.Q is still consumed by Section 0b -- it is cleared and
           logged as 'START pressed' -- so a press here is visible in the log
           and simply has no effect, exactly like a press during a cycle. *)

    E_MasterAutoStep.NOT_HOMED:
        (* In Automatic but NOT armed: entered on power-up (stHmi.eStep's
           declared default), on any time spent in Manual, and on operator STOP.
           nStateOut reports 40, which the robot treats as "wait" -- it is
           neither IDLE(0) "send me a bulb" nor ERR(99) "reset me".

           Nothing is driven and no timer runs (Section 3 falls to its ELSE, as
           for IDLE). The ONLY exit is an operator START -- the HMI button or the
           green PB3, which MAIN maps onto the same stHmi.bStart field. *)
        ResetAllCommands();

        stHmi.iErrorCode := 0;
        stHmi.sErrorText := '';

        (* bTempTestRun self-arms, so a soak needs no operator START at all: tick
           the flag online and the machine homes and then cycles.

           THIS IS THE TERM THAT MAKES THE GATE NECESSARY. Skipping the START
           press also skips the operator confirming they are clear of the
           machine, so on a Manual -> Auto return it re-opens the hazard the
           arming model closed. Compile-time FALSE unless bBenchBuild is set.

           No log line here: this fires once per enable, and the enable WARN is
           the entry immediately before it with Section 3's
           NOT_HOMED -> INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING right after. *)
        IF bMachineAuto AND (fbTrigStart.Q OR bTempTestRun) THEN
            bHomeThenIdle := TRUE;
            stHmi.eStep   := E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING:
        (* Mirror end-of-cycle PUSH_RETRACTING: assert push retract only. Sep
           stays uncommanded (whatever position it was left in — its retract
           follows in INIT_SEP_RETRACTING). Grip uncommanded here. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bPushCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllPushRetracted OR fbSim_InitPushRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_SEP_RETRACTING;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 6;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_SEP_RETRACTING:
        (* Push is confirmed home; now retract sep. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllSepRetracted OR fbSim_InitSepRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_GRIP_RETRACTING;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 7;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'INIT_SEP_RETRACTING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.INIT_GRIP_RETRACTING:
        (* Both Sep and Push confirmed home; now retract grip. Defensive
           symmetry with INIT_PUSH_RETRACTING / INIT_SEP_RETRACTING so a
           prior manual jog does not leave grip clamped when the plate
           arrives. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllGripRetracted OR fbSim_InitGripRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            (* End of the shared retract chain -- the one place the two callers
               diverge. Homing (operator START, or ERR recovery) stops here with
               the machine armed; a robot-started cycle carries on into the bulb
               sequence. Everything above this line is identical for both. *)
            IF bHomeThenIdle THEN
                stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.IDLE;
            ELSE
                stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.CHECK_PLATE;
            END_IF;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 8;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'INIT_GRIP_RETRACTING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.CHECK_PLATE:
        (* Nothing driven — waiting for the robot to place the plate on the
           fixture. Both plate sensors must confirm TRUE before advancing.
           No sensorless fallback: the whole point of this state is to gate
           on the plate presence. Timeout uses tPlateWaitTimeoutMs (see
           Section 3 timer selector). *)
        ResetAllCommands();

        IF bPlateOk OR fbSim_WaitPlate.Q THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.GRIP_EXTENDING;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            (* Bypass removes only the ERROR, not the wait. The sensor
               fast-path above still applies, so with working sensors the
               cycle is unchanged; with unwired or faulty ones
               tPlateWaitTimeoutMs degrades into a fixed placement dwell and
               we carry on. That mirrors how bNoSensors turns tStepTimeoutMs
               into a fixed step duration, and it keeps the plate gate honest
               by default -- bNoSensors deliberately does NOT skip this
               state, so bypassing it has to be an explicit, separate
               decision. *)
            IF stCfg.bBypassPlateSensors THEN
                stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.GRIP_EXTENDING;
            ELSE
                stHmi.iErrorCode := 9;
                stHmi.sErrorText := 'CHECK_PLATE timed out (plate never arrived)';
                stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
            END_IF;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.GRIP_EXTENDING:
        (* Plate is on the fixture; clamp it from both sides. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllGripExtended OR fbSim_GripExt.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_EXTENDING;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 10;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'GRIP_EXTENDING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_EXTENDING:
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;   (* hold grip clamped *)
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllSepExtended OR fbSim_SepExt.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            (* Skip the former WAIT_POS2 — the robot has already moved clear
               of the stand before signaling start via TCP CMD:1. *)
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_EXTENDING;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN   (* only reachable when NOT bNoSensors *)
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 1;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'SEP_EXTENDING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_EXTENDING:
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdExtend[i]  := TRUE;   (* hold sep out *)
            bPushCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllPushExtended OR fbSim_PushExt.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.DWELL_PUSH;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 3;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'PUSH_EXTENDING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.DWELL_PUSH:
        (* Hold everything out. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdExtend[i]  := TRUE;
            bPushCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF tonStep.Q OR fbSim_DwellPush.Q THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_RETRACTING;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_RETRACTING:
        (* Sep still extended, push retract command asserted. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdExtend[i]   := TRUE;
            bPushCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllPushRetracted OR fbSim_PushRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_RETRACTED_DWELL;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 4;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'PUSH_RETRACTING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.PUSH_RETRACTED_DWELL:
        (* Sep still held extended, push commands cleared. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF tonStep.Q OR fbSim_PushRetDwell.Q THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_RETRACTING;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_RETRACTING:
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllSepRetracted OR fbSim_SepRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_RETRACTED_DWELL;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 5;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'SEP_RETRACTING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.SEP_RETRACTED_DWELL:
        (* Sep/Push home; grip still clamped (released in GRIP_RETRACTING). *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdExtend[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF tonStep.Q OR fbSim_SepRetDwell.Q THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.GRIP_RETRACTING;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.GRIP_RETRACTING:
        (* Very last motion: release the plate. On successful retract,
           increment nCyclesCompleted and return to IDLE. *)
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllGripRetracted OR fbSim_GripRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.nCyclesCompleted := stHmi.nCyclesCompleted + 1;
            (* Back to IDLE -- still ARMED, so the robot's next CMD:1 (or an
               operator START) runs the next bulb. Nothing re-arms by itself. *)
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.IDLE;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 11;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'GRIP_RETRACTING timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    (* ---- FAULT-RECOVERY RETRACT CHAIN (2026-08-05) ------------------
       Same motion and the same collision ordering as the RETRACT/INIT
       chain -- push, then sep, then grip -- but with its own identity, so
       the panel, the log and the wire can all tell a failed RECOVERY from
       a failed ARMING. Entered only from ERR on RESET; always exits to
       IDLE, because at that point the machine really is homed.
       ----------------------------------------------------------------- *)
    E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_PUSH_RETR:
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bPushCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllPushRetracted OR fbSim_RecoverPushRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_SEP_RETR;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 12;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'RECOVER push retract timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_SEP_RETR:
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 3 DO
            bSepCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllSepRetracted OR fbSim_RecoverSepRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_GRIP_RETR;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 13;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'RECOVER sep retract timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.RECOVER_GRIP_RETR:
        ResetAllCommands();
        FOR i := 1 TO 2 DO
            bGripCmdRetract[i] := TRUE;
        END_FOR;

        IF bAllGripRetracted OR fbSim_RecoverGripRet.Q OR (stCfg.bNoSensors AND tonStep.Q) THEN
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.IDLE;
        ELSIF tonStep.Q THEN
            stHmi.iErrorCode := 14;
            stHmi.sErrorText := 'RECOVER grip retract timed out';
            stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.ERR;
        END_IF;

    E_MasterAutoStep.ERR:
        (* Latched. nStateOut mirrors ERR(99) so the robot's TCP client
           sees the fault via STATE:99 and can invoke its own error
           handler. Exit only via Section 2 (HMI RESET or CMD:2). *)
        ResetAllCommands();

ELSE
    (* Defensive: any eStep with no branch above lands here, and it snaps to
       NOT_HOMED -- NOT IDLE. IDLE is wire value 0, the one value that makes
       the robot send CMD:1, and this path is reached with the pistons in
       unknown positions. It is genuinely reachable: GVL_HMI carries
       TcHmiSymbol.AddSymbol so eStep is writable over ADS, and WAIT_POS2(2)
       is a declared enumerator with no branch. NOT_HOMED reports 'wait' and
       requires a human START. *)
    ResetAllCommands();
    stHmi.eStep := E_MasterAutoStep.NOT_HOMED;
END_CASE;

(* ==========================================
   5. STATUS MIRROR — human-readable step name + running flag
   TO_STRING relies on the enum's {to_string} attribute.
   bAnyRunning is TRUE any time the cycle is actively driving pistons —
   MAIN uses it to force enabled pistons into Automatic mode. False in ERR
   so the operator's manual choice is preserved after a fault.

   The "PLC is running" signal to the robot is derived robot-side from
   nStateOut != IDLE (see FB_RobotTcpClient); no separate outbound field
   needed here.
   ========================================== *)
stHmi.sStepText := TO_STRING(stHmi.eStep);
bAnyRunning     := (stHmi.eStep <> E_MasterAutoStep.IDLE)
               AND (stHmi.eStep <> E_MasterAutoStep.ERR);
]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
    <Method Name="ResetAllCommands" Id="{cce2d1ba-31f4-5860-90e9-54ebd0bd9b9f}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(* Drive every piston command FALSE.

   Called at the top of EVERY state in Section 4, which then re-asserts only
   the commands it actually owns. That replaces the previous mix of explicit
   clears and commands held by omission -- eight of sixteen states cleared
   the full set and the rest inherited FALSE from whichever state ran before,
   so reading one branch in isolation could not tell you what the coils were
   doing.

   Behaviour is unchanged: each old body's net effect was already
   'everything FALSE except the commands it listed'. This makes that
   explicit, and makes a forgotten clear impossible.

   NOTE it clears COMMANDS, not coils. FB_SolSpringPiston_2Pos RETAINS its
   coil when no command branch matches, so clearing commands mid-stroke holds
   the piston where it is rather than dropping it. That is exactly why every
   state has to re-assert what it wants, every scan.

   Uses its own loop counter -- the FB's `i` belongs to the caller. *)
METHOD ResetAllCommands
VAR
    k : INT;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[FOR k := 1 TO 3 DO
    bSepCmdExtend[k]   := FALSE;
    bSepCmdRetract[k]  := FALSE;
    bPushCmdExtend[k]  := FALSE;
    bPushCmdRetract[k] := FALSE;
END_FOR;
FOR k := 1 TO 2 DO
    bGripCmdExtend[k]  := FALSE;
    bGripCmdRetract[k] := FALSE;
END_FOR;
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <LineIds Name="FB_MasterAutoCycle">
      <LineId Id="3" Count="526" />
      <LineId Id="2" Count="0" />
    </LineIds>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>